<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Samples the vnode free list once a minute, because the panic in #82 destroys
  the evidence for its own cause. The kernel says `vnode: table is full` while
  it is happening and none of that survives the reboot: the unified log kept
  zero kernel messages from the panicked boot session, on 2026-08-26 and
  2026-08-27 alike, while user-space entries from the same minutes came back
  fine. So the post-mortem could not say which table ran out, only that
  something returned ENFILE.

  kern.free_vnodes is the number the kernel prints as `0 free` in that message,
  so watching it fall answers that question before the next panic rather than
  after it — and the sampler names who is holding files while it falls, which
  is the half a post-mortem cannot reconstruct at all.

  What it records, and why those four counters, is in the script:
  ~/.local/bin/vnode-watch.sh (os/macos/.local/bin/vnode-watch.sh here).

  Not a `log stream` on the kernel message: that only fires once the table is
  already full, which is the moment the machine is about to die. This sees the
  approach.

  ponytail: append-only, no rotation. Roughly 200 KB a day between the two
  logs, and the whole thing goes away once #82 is settled — `launchctl bootout
  gui/$(id -u)/local.vnode-watch`, then delete this file, the script and both
  logs.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>Label</key>
	<string>local.vnode-watch</string>
	<key>ProgramArguments</key>
	<array>
		<!-- Through sh, and no StandardOutPath: launchd expands neither $HOME
		     nor a leading ~, and an absolute /Users/... is true on exactly one
		     machine — which is the thing claude-settings-test exists to catch. -->
		<string>/bin/sh</string>
		<string>-c</string>
		<string>exec "$HOME/.local/bin/vnode-watch.sh"</string>
	</array>
	<key>RunAtLoad</key>
	<true/>
	<key>StartInterval</key>
	<integer>60</integer>
</dict>
</plist>
